G15 / Route B patch 0012 — target→pool identity, derived not supplied.
=======================================================================

ONE CONCEPTUAL CHANGE: derive the pool location from TARGET IDENTITY instead of
accepting a fixture-specific pool location as input.

WHY. `pool_offset` had a CONSUMER (`assert_result_consumed.sh --pool-offset`) and
no producer. The embedder supplied `0xd4a8` when the selector was the literal
string `"RouteBThing.value"` — an offset read off RELEASE 26's disassembly, for
one fixture's target — so the field echoed its own argument for one selector and
reported NOT_REQUESTED for every other specimen, including every G15 arm. That is
why arm A could not identify its own call site. See
`evidence/g15/routeb_caller_scan_gap.txt`.

WHAT CHANGED

  1. The `0xd4a8` / `RouteBThing.value` special case is DELETED, not disabled.
     It was a live landmine: any later specimen with that selector would have had
     `IndexFromOffset(0xd4a8)` read against whatever release was running and a
     confident wrong identity reported.

  2. A DERIVED target→pool scan is the producer of the location. It scans the
     global object pool for `entry.ptr() == target.ptr()`, needs no caller name
     and no constant, and works for every target.

  3. MULTIPLICITY IS PRESERVED. The scan does not stop at the first hit:
       0 matches  -> TPOOL_ABSENT   (a RESULT — this is what an inlined callee
                                     looks like, so it must not be an error code)
       1 match    -> TPOOL_UNIQUE   (index and offset are an identity)
       >1 matches -> TPOOL_AMBIGUOUS(first and second reported; the reader is not
                                     allowed to resolve it by optimism)
     Only UNIQUE licenses reading the index as identity. The offset comes from
     `ObjectPool::OffsetFromIndex`, the VM's own rule, symmetric with the
     `IndexFromOffset` the assertion path uses — never layout arithmetic.

  4. The supplied-offset path is DEMOTED to an independent assertion: *does this
     slot hold the target?* It is no longer a source of `pool_offset`.

  5. The six-state CALLER scan is untouched and unconflated. Two instruments:
     the caller scan answers about a NAMED caller on request; the target-pool
     scan supplies the upstream measurement.

  6. Trace version bumped v4 → v5, so a v4 reader cannot mistake the absence of
     `tpool_*` for a measurement.

THE NEGATIVE CONTROL THIS PATCH OWES, AND DOES NOT YET HAVE
------------------------------------------------------------
Required: feed the historical `0xd4a8` as a supplied offset on a release where it
points at a VALID BUT DIFFERENT object, and require the assertion path to report
`POOL_ENTRY_NOT_FUNCTION` or `pool_entry_equals_target=0` — never a confident
identity — while the DERIVED scan independently reports the true location. That
proves the two instruments disagree in the right direction and that the demotion
is real.

It is not built here because it needs a device run or a host harness that can
attach a patch, and this patch is host-compile-verified only. **Do not read a
green `tpool_status` in the field as evidence that the assertion path was
demoted correctly until that control exists.**

BUILD STATUS AT CAPTURE
------------------------
`obj/flutter/shell/common/shorebird/shorebird.shorebird.o` and
`obj/flutter/third_party/dart/runtime/lib/libdart_lib_aotruntime_product.object.o`
both compile clean, zero errors. No iOS engine build, no cell, no device run.
Changes NOTHING about arm A: `OLD-kill` stays unexplained, `routeBValue`'s
consumption stays UNMEASURED, and the target is unchanged.

